INSERT INTO audit_units (slug_key, title, disposition, new_url, new_text, sort_order) VALUES ('episcopalian', 'What Is an Episcopalian?', 'rewritten', '/final/connect/episcopalian/', 'A Way of Following Jesus

What is an Episcopalian ?

Ancient prayer, open table, a Church that loves questions as much as answers.

The Episcopal Church is sometimes called a bridge between the Catholic and Protestant traditions. We hold to ancient liturgy and the sacraments, and we read scripture with curiosity, not fear. Our Sunday worship is shaped by music, the Word, prayer, and Holy Communion: a sacred meal that remembers the supper Jesus shared with his disciples.

We are part of the worldwide Anglican Communion. Our parish is rooted in the Episcopal Diocese of North Carolina, and through our diocese we are connected to congregations on every continent.

Everyone is welcome at the table

There are no prerequisites in the Episcopal Church. We welcome people of every age, gender, sexual orientation, race, economic background, and life circumstance. If you are baptized in any Christian tradition, you are welcome to receive Communion with us. If you are not, you are welcome to come forward for a blessing, or to remain in your seat, no pressure either way.

A faith that holds room for questions

Episcopalians follow Jesus Christ. We treat scripture with deep seriousness and read it with intellectual honesty, including the parts that are difficult or that pull against each other. The tradition we inherit is sometimes called the "via media", the middle way: we make room for disagreement and dialogue, knowing every person at the table is beloved of God.

Common prayer is what binds us

We pray from the Book of Common Prayer, a book of liturgies first compiled in 1549 and revised many times since. It means that on a given Sunday, Episcopalians around the world are praying the same words. You don''t need to agree on every doctrine to belong here. You need only to come and pray together.

Faith that shows up in the neighborhood

In our baptismal covenant, we promise to seek and serve Christ in all people, to respect the dignity of every human being, and to work for justice and peace. That promise shapes everything from our outreach partnerships in Concord to the way we treat each other on a Sunday morning.

Learn More

Want to dig deeper ?

The wider Episcopal Church and the Diocese of North Carolina both have helpful introductions. Or come visit. Sunday morning is the best way to know if this tradition fits.

Plan a Visit
The Episcopal Church
Diocese of NC

Questions are always welcome

If something here resonates, or if you''d like to ask a question that the page didn''t answer, send us a note.

Email Us
Plan a Visit', 5);
INSERT INTO audit_units (slug_key, title, disposition, new_url, new_text, sort_order) VALUES ('life-events', 'Life Events', 'merged', '/final/connect/life-events/', 'Weddings · Baptisms · Funerals

Life Events at All Saints'' Episcopal , Concord NC

The moments that mark a life, held in prayer, music, and the company of a parish that knows your name.

Marriage

Weddings at All Saints'' Episcopal

An Episcopal wedding is more than a ceremony. It is a sacrament: a public promise made before God and a community that commits to walk with you. The liturgy is rooted in the Book of Common Prayer, with room for music, scripture, and personal touches that fit you.

We ask that at least one partner be a member of All Saints'' before a wedding is scheduled, and we take marriage preparation seriously. Our clergy meet with every couple through a preparation process that''s honest, warm, and practical.

Reach out early. We''d rather start the conversation about membership and timing well before a date is set.

Contact Us to Begin

Baptism

Baptisms at All Saints'' Episcopal

In the Episcopal Church, baptism is a sacrament, a visible sign of the grace already at work in a life. Through baptism, the person being baptized (an infant, a child, a teen, or an adult) is welcomed fully into the Body of Christ and into the life of this parish.

Baptisms typically happen on principal feast days throughout the year: the Baptism of Our Lord, the Easter Vigil, Pentecost, and All Saints'' Sunday. We''ll walk with parents, godparents, and adult candidates through a short preparation conversation before the day.

If you were baptized as an infant and want to publicly own that faith for yourself, talk to us about confirmation. The bishop visits the parish each year and welcomes those ready to take that step.

Contact Us to Begin

Funerals & Memorials

Funerals at All Saints'' Episcopal

The Burial Office in the Book of Common Prayer is built on Christian hope: that the life we share with God in this world is not ended at death. The liturgy is honest about grief and steady in its trust. It holds families through one of the hardest days of a life.

All Saints'' clergy guide families through every part of planning a service, including readings, hymns, and the role of the parish in the days surrounding the funeral. The parish often provides a reception for families afterward.

If you are facing the loss of someone you love, or thinking ahead about your own service, please reach out. We are here.

Contact Us to Begin

Whatever the moment, we''re here for it

A wedding, a baptism, a goodbye. Send a note. We''ll meet you where you are.

Email the Parish Office
