How we learn , together

Christian formation as a lifelong practice, not a program.

Faith, deepening

Formation is the whole life

Christian formation is the lifelong process of growing in relationship with God, with ourselves, with each other, and with all of creation. It happens in classrooms and in cars, at the altar and at the kitchen table. At All Saints'', it shows up three ways on Sunday mornings, and one way that takes four years.

Children & Youth Formation

Three layered offerings on Sunday mornings, plus monthly youth gatherings.

Nursery (ages 0–3): professionally staffed by Katie, Jane, and Kristen. Additional coverage for ages 3–5 coming soon.

Elementary Sunday School (10:00 AM, Education Wing): uses the Dig-In curriculum from Group Publishing, supplemented with units on Advent and Lent.

Middle School "Breakfast with Jesus" (Youth Room): a lectionary-based study produced in-house.

High School "Coffee with Jesus" (Panera): honest conversation about making faith matter Monday through Saturday.

EYC (Episcopal Youth Community): middle and high schoolers gathering for monthly "Invite a Friend!" socials, diocesan retreats, local service, and annual trips.

Adult Formation

A standing Sunday class, in person and on Zoom, with topics that rotate through the program year.

When: most Sundays, 10:10–11:00 AM

Where: in person at the church and live on Zoom

Format: rotating studies through scripture, theology, church history, and contemporary questions

Check the Weekly Word for the current study and the Zoom link, or email the office and we''ll get you in.

Education for Ministry (EfM)

Every baptized person is called to ministry. EfM is the four-year program that takes that seriously.

Offered under the auspices of the University of the South (Sewanee, TN)

Four years of study in scripture, church history, and theology

Strong emphasis on integrating faith into daily life through theological reflection

All Saints'' currently hosts two EfM groups

A short video introduction is available, ask the office for the link

Have questions about formation ?

Tell us what age, what topic, or what time of week works, and we''ll point you to the right room or the right person.

Get in Touch
Email the Office', 9);
INSERT INTO audit_units (slug_key, title, disposition, new_url, new_text, sort_order) VALUES ('clergy', 'Clergy & Staff', 'merged', '/final/clergy/', 'Our Clergy

Clergy

The priests who preach, preside, and walk with us through ordinary weeks and the harder ones

Interim Rector

The Rev. John Johanssen

Fr. John began as our Interim Rector on January 1, 2026. A native of Ohio, he has served faithfully in several parishes throughout his ordained ministry. Before responding to the call to priesthood, he practiced law for several years.

He received his Master of Divinity from Virginia Theological Seminary and has served in a variety of roles, including previous interim work.

Specialized training in interim ministry, equipping him to guide congregations with care and clarity during seasons of transition.

Fun fact: Fr. John''s hobbies include music, sailing, hiking, and any activity in nature.

Email Fr. John

Fr. Jim Bernacki

Photo coming soon

Priest Associate

The Rev. Dr. James B. ("Father Jim") Bernacki

Fr. Jim joined the All Saints'' staff in the summer of 2019. A native of the Buffalo, New York area, he graduated from Canisius College with a B.A. in Philosophy and went on to receive an Ed.M. in Secondary Math Education from SUNY Buffalo. With Permanent Certification from the State of New York, he taught math at primary, secondary, and college levels.

He received an M.Div. in Theology from Christ the King Seminary in East Aurora, NY, and a D.Min. in Anglican Studies from Seabury-Western Theological Seminary. Ordained to the priesthood in 1979, he served in Roman Catholic parishes in the Buffalo area until he was received into the Episcopal Church in 1988, when he married his wife Sandy.

He later served congregations in New York, Ohio, and West Virginia, most recently as Rector for Christ Episcopal Church in Albemarle, NC, for over 15 years until retiring in 2018.

At All Saints'', Fr. Jim coordinates a team of members who lead Compline every Tuesday at 8 p.m. online, leads or preaches at Sunday and weekday services, and provides pastoral care and visitation.

Email Fr. Jim

Reach out

Whether you need a moment of prayer, a pastoral conversation, or just have a question about a service, the door is open. Send a prayer request or email the parish office, and we will get back to you.

Submit a Prayer Request
Email the Office', 10);
INSERT INTO audit_units (slug_key, title, disposition, new_url, new_text, sort_order) VALUES ('history', 'History', 'rewritten', '/final/history/', 'Since 1878

Our history

Nearly 150 years of worship, mergers, missions, and ministry in Cabarrus County. The story is still being written.

All Saints'' has been part of Concord since before the town had paved streets. What started as a handful of families gathered for prayer became a parish, weathered a merger, built a campus, and sent its people out into the world.

"The Episcopal branch of the Jesus Movement, rooted in Concord since 1878."

A Walk Through the Years

Milestones along the way

Founding, building, merging, sending. Each year here is a real moment in the life of this parish.

1877

A first baptism, a first rector

On November 27, Elizabeth C. Gibson became the first Episcopalian baptized in Cabarrus County. The Rev. John Thomas Wheat, D.D., became the first rector.

1886

A first building, bought used

The young congregation bought the old Presbyterian church for $500. A modest start, and a roof of their own.

1891

Land at Cabarrus and Depot

Property at the corner of Cabarrus Avenue and Depot Street (now Spring Street) was purchased for $300. That corner would be home for the next 81 years.

1893

Officially a congregation

All Saints'' was officially recognized as a congregation by the Diocese of North Carolina, formally joining the wider church.

1941

St. James joins the Diocese

A separate group of Episcopalians in Kannapolis was admitted into the Diocese as the Congregation of St. James on July 10. Two congregations, ten miles apart.

1953

St. James cornerstone

Bishop Edwin Penick presided at the cornerstone laying of St. James Church on November 7.

1954

First service in Kannapolis

The first service was held in St. James'' new building on April 4.

1968

A resolution to merge

On December 8, both Concord and Kannapolis congregations read a resolution to merge. Two parishes considering becoming one.

1969

One parish, All Saints''

On January 1, the merger was voted and approved. Two churches became one parish: All Saints''.

1971

Miss Jen turns the first shovel

Groundbreaking for the present church building on April 8. Mrs. J.L. Brown, "Miss Jen," the senior communicant, turned the first shovel of dirt.

1972

A new home on Lake Concord Road

The last service in the old Spring Street building was held on June 30. The first service in the present building took place on August 6 with the placement of the Foundation Stone. Bishop Thomas A. Fraser dedicated the Church and Parish House on October 22.

1984

Governor''s Volunteer Award

In June, All Saints'' received the Governor''s Volunteer Award for exemplary community involvement. Recognition for service that had already been quiet practice for years.

1996

Haiti CODEP begins

All Saints'' began its long-running Haiti mission with CODEP, reforestation work in the Cormeir valley. Three decades on, the trees are still going in.

2014

A preschool on campus

The Lockhart Child Development Center (LCDC) opened on the All Saints'' campus, a Cabarrus County Schools partnership serving 36 pre-K students.

2026

A new interim rector

The Rev. John Johanssen began as Interim Rector on January 1, walking with the parish through its rector search and into whatever comes next.

"We are part of a story that began long before us and continues long after. The work of loving God and loving our neighbor is not yet finished."

All Saints'' Episcopal Church · 1878 to today', 11);
